WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.590
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.500
- Well, welcome to another
episode of the "Addy Hour."

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.780
Today it's my immense pleasure to be able

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.170
to welcome Dr. Michael Eric
Dyson to the "Addy Hour"

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.150
for a conversation about race,
community, and mental health.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.060
This is someone who obviously
needs no introduction

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.321
but I'm gonna go ahead and
give a brief one anyway.

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:23.154
(Michael laughs)

00:00:23.154 --> 00:00:26.480
Dr. Dyson is the Centennial
Chair at Vanderbilt University

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.770
and also a University
Distinguished Professor

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.200
of African American and Diaspora Studies

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.990
in the Colleges of Arts and Sciences

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.230
and a University Distinguished Professor

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.750
of Ethics and Society
in the Divinity School.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:39.880
He's someone who also

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.390
is a "New York Times"
contributing opinion writer,

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.670
also contributing editor at "New Republic"

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.780
and ESPN's "The Undefeated,"
has written numerous books,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.670
has another book coming out very soon,

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.230
which we'll probably talk
about on this podcast as well,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.390
and he's someone who has won
lots of prestigious awards

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.050
including an American Book
Award, two NAACP Image Awards,

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.687
and "Ebony" has also cited him as one

00:01:01.687 --> 00:01:04.870
of the 100 most influential
African Americans

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.970
and one of the 150 most
powerful blacks in the nation.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.530
So someone who continues to
contribute to public discourse

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.100
and public debate on
places like "The Late Show"

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.177
with Stephen Colbert, "Real
Time with Bill Maher,"

00:01:16.177 --> 00:01:18.577
"Good Morning America," "The Today Show,"

00:01:18.577 --> 00:01:20.337
"All Things Considered," "Tavis Smiley,"

00:01:20.337 --> 00:01:22.860
"Def Poetry Jam," the list goes on and on.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.690
But all that to say is
that I'm very grateful

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.440
that he's taken time out
of his busy schedule.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.560
Dr. Dyson, I really
appreciate you being here

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.770
on the "Addy Hour" podcast and welcome.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:31.603
- Thank you, sir.

00:01:31.603 --> 00:01:34.750
It's always great to be with
a distinguished intellectual

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.200
and, you know, influencer like yourself.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.020
So thanks for having me on.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.010
- Oh, of course, and I
appreciate those kind words.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.320
I'm really excited
about this conversation.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.720
So again, you know, just with the,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.400
all the impact that you have in your place

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.900
as such a distinguished
scholar and cultural critic,

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.130
a Baptist preacher, activist, influencer,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.490
a lot of the topics that you touch on

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.430
are really near and dear
to the conversations

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.610
that we have on this podcast as well,

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.700
especially as we think about
how aspects of race, racism,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.090
social justice, and
equity impact and interact

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.540
with mental health in this country.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:08.870
So I just wanted to start out, you know,

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.640
my listeners know I like to just check in

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.290
and see how people are doing,
so in the midst of everything

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.480
that's going on with the
ongoing pandemic, with COVID,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.260
with the ongoing systemic
racial injustices,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.030
even as we are preparing
for the start of the trial

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.930
for Ahmaud Arbery, the murder trial,

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.780
just wanted to ask how you're
doing in this current moment

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.310
in general with everything going on.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:29.550
- Well, thanks for asking.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.630
Yeah, it is a bit much for
all of us to deal with,

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:36.060
with COVID, with the global pandemic,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.540
with the racial reckoning
that we're undergoing still,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.637
the trial of Ahmaud Arbery,

00:02:40.637 --> 00:02:44.557
and I write about him in my book,

00:02:44.557 --> 00:02:47.670
"Long Time Coming: Reckoning
with Race in America."

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.160
So it's a lot for us as a
community but I'm holding up,

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.850
holding up the bloodstained
banner of truth

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.880
and commitment to the faith

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.860
that my ancestors have passed on to me,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.470
that my role models have given to me.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.790
So I'm in good shape

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.250
and glad to be here with you this morning.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.590
- Excellent. Well, I appreciate that.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.090
And just the importance of the path

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.204
that's been blazed before
us from the ancestors.

00:03:12.204 --> 00:03:14.860
I appreciate you mentioning that as well.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.480
The other thing I wanted to ask, you know,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.117
I heard one of your recent interviews

00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:21.440
on "The Breakfast Club" when
you were just talking about,

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.430
you know, coming up and
not necessarily thinking

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.290
about self-care per se in
how you went about things,

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.310
but, you know, just thinking
about who you are as a person,

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.810
all the conversations that
you're continually involved in,

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.140
a lot of heavy topics, how
do you manage all of that

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.210
in the midst of just trying to think

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.220
about your own emotional wellness

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.460
and care in the midst of that?

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.630
- Yes, sir, well, I credit
the younger generation

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:46.143
with giving us, your generation
and even those younger,

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.380
with forcing us to confront this.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.400
You know, we avoided it.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.910
We were traumatized, re-traumatized,

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.290
felt that we had to go on regardless,

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:00.060
and I think there's something
to be said for that mindset,

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.660
but there's also something
to be said for reckoning

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.750
with your own internal clock,
your rhythm, your cadence,

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.750
your flow, your mental health,
your stability, your outlook,

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.750
your anxiety meter,
your depression measure,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.260
because we're all bombarded
relentlessly and constantly

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.750
from both external stimuli and forces

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.780
and internal realities, that
we confront perceptions,

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.780
thoughts, emotions, memories
that haunt us, that trigger us.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:40.020
So it is extremely necessary
to do that kind of inventory.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.820
You know, I like sports events, going out

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.027
and looking at basketball and
football and watching movies.

00:04:48.027 --> 00:04:52.840
(laughs) So those are some of
the things that keep me going,

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.170
keep me refreshed and the like,

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.160
and listening to music and so on,

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.690
going to a concert and so on.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.600
Those kinds of things
are extremely important,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.710
trying to get in an exercise
regimen, get in better shape,

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.820
you know, 'cause you gotta
be here in order to be here.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.730
- Yeah, yeah, that's true.
- And

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.490
so I think the younger
generation has reminded us

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.670
of the politics of self-care

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.280
and the necessity of engaging it

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.220
without the kind of guilt
that older people did,

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:27.220
without lapsing into some
of the extremes of self-care

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.930
'cause if self-care comes
at the expense of other care

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:34.230
or myopic narcissistic self-preoccupation

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.570
at the expense of commitment
to the larger society.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:41.820
Was it good for Martin Luther
King Jr. to do all he did?

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.700
If he was really
concerned about self-care,

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.160
would he have marched on some days?

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:45.993
No.

00:05:45.993 --> 00:05:48.160
Would he have spoken
on some other days? No.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.050
So you got to balance out, you know,

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:53.370
a commitment to a higher
purpose and a greater goal

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:58.050
with also the recognition
that you've got to be here

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.750
and in good space and spirits and body

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.830
and in health and mind in order

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.340
to render the best service possible.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.249
- Yeah, that's so good,

00:06:06.249 --> 00:06:07.700
and it sounds like you have
so many of those pieces

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.890
just baked in to what
you do on a daily basis.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.316
I think it's really keeping you,

00:06:12.316 --> 00:06:13.900
you touched on the
generational difference too

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:15.500
and I even see that myself, you know,

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.848
even with younger trainees,
they're coming up,

00:06:17.848 --> 00:06:19.010
it seems like they're so invested

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.010
in so many different things,

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.870
which I felt like in some
ways I kind of waited

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.820
till I got to a certain level of stature

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.070
before I really jumped into
some of those passions,

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.930
even though those passions were there.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.660
How was that process for
you in terms of, you know,

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.370
developing your passions
and pursuing them?

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.080
How did that come about and
how'd you get to this place

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.700
in your career being so involved

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.190
in so many different spheres of influence?

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:40.670
- Well, as they say in basketball,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.076
I took what the defense gave me.

00:06:42.076 --> 00:06:44.060
(both laughing)

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.670
So it's what opportunities

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.380
and obstacles presented themselves,

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.130
some doors opening, other doors closing,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:53.933
and some windows being lifted
and some being nailed shut.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.220
It doesn't mean that I
have a fatalistic outlook

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.690
and perspective that suggests
that if it's difficult,

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.290
therefore it's not for
me, that's not the case,

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.540
but if you keep banging
your head against the window

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:07.010
and knocking against the
door and it ain't open

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.690
and then consider opening another door,

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.560
considering that door ain't for you,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.650
considering that God might
be looking out for you,

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:16.294
what you asking for ain't
what you need to have

00:07:16.294 --> 00:07:18.560
without being paternalistic

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.050
or masking human authoritarianism

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.060
with an appeal to, you
know, God's Providence.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:27.490
But at the end of the day,

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:29.420
you got to have a sense of connection

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:34.180
and spiritual attunement so
that you are constantly praying

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:38.140
for the best path, looking
for the best opportunity,

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:42.880
trying to create the
next level of investment

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:44.660
and the return on that investment

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.950
and what your spiritual
growth and your career growth

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:49.010
and your professional growth

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.380
and your vocational growth will be.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.030
So for me, it's been a measure

00:07:56.030 --> 00:08:00.360
of trying to match my goals and my gifts.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.800
What goals are dictated by my gifts?

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.340
Now, you might want to
be a basketball player.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:08.873
There are some who are my
height, maybe 5'10", you know,

00:08:08.873 --> 00:08:11.574
maybe I-
- There's some good examples.

00:08:11.574 --> 00:08:12.407
- What was that? (laughs)

00:08:12.407 --> 00:08:13.369
- There's some good examples

00:08:13.369 --> 00:08:14.570
at your height.
- There's some good examples

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.840
but the fact that they're relatively few,

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.570
which suggests that that ain't, you know,

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.547
the likelihood as
opposed to a person 6'5",

00:08:22.547 --> 00:08:25.890
6'10", and so on.

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:27.830
So, or what are your skills

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.560
and what are your intuitions
generated as a result of?

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.710
And so when I look at what
my intuitional base is,

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:39.250
it's premised on an intellectual
engagement with the world,

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.320
an analysis of things as they are,

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.350
a kind of spiritual connection to them

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.710
as an ordained minister,
as an intellectual,

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:50.150
as a professor, you know,
I develop those talents.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:52.400
So thinking and reflecting and writing

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.230
and engaging the world.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:56.060
And as a result of that, doors opened.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.250
Opportunities presented themselves.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:02.947
And I began to reinforce the
value of my self-perception

00:09:02.947 --> 00:09:07.340
and linking my goals and my gifts.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:11.150
And I think if more of us
would link our goals and gifts,

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.930
we would understand, you know,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:17.930
how it is that what God has
placed in us can be elicited,

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.380
can be evoked, can be brought out

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:24.110
as a result of whatever circumstances,

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:27.320
opportunities, or obstacles
present themselves

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:31.030
that, you know, encourage us, coerce us

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:32.800
to deal with what's around us.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:35.486
So, you know, my pastor
coming into my church

00:09:35.486 --> 00:09:37.084
when I was 12 years old,

00:09:37.084 --> 00:09:42.084
me entering a oratorical
contest when I was 11 years old,

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.190
being asked to speak parts in church

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.380
when I was younger than that,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.660
my fifth grade teacher, Mrs. James,

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.230
teaching us about black history

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.542
when it wasn't popular in 1968-

00:09:53.542 --> 00:09:54.600
- Wow, that's saying something.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.220
- And winning a blue ribbon
for reciting the poetry

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:00.310
of Paul Laurence Dunbar
and on and on and on.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:03.190
So my gifts matched, you know,

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.420
and dictated to a degree my goals.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.210
If this is my skillset, if
these are my gifts and talents,

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.760
what do I use them to realize

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.510
and what ambitions do I nurture
in my own heart and mind

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:20.240
in order to find that relationship

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:22.290
between my gifts and my goals

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.670
and that allow me to
live a satisfying life?

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.060
So that's how I've
tried to play it so far.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.360
- Yeah. That's really powerful.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.193
And the fact that you remember

00:10:29.193 --> 00:10:31.360
your fifth grade teacher's
name, that's saying something.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.200
- Oh man, she was extremely important,

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.170
life-changing, you know?

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.730
My mother told me later that
many of the black parents,

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:40.890
'cause I'm in a segregated school

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.720
even though I'm living
up north in Detroit,

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.210
we had a white principal,
Mrs. Christianson, I remember,

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.820
but we had black teachers and black staff

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:50.750
and everything else was
black in that school,

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.400
including the students.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.280
And my mother said they
went to Mrs. Christianson

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.760
and complained to her,
some of the black parents,

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:02.740
about the black fifth
grade teacher, Mrs. James.

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:05.690
What are they going to do
with this black history stuff?

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:07.470
This is not part of the curriculum.

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:10.790
Well, I'll tell you, I'm a
PhD from Princeton University

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:14.690
and I've written 25 books
and I write about politics

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.040
and race and culture and religion

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.810
and all the stuff that she taught us then.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.420
So Mrs. James, your efforts paid off.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.490
- Wow, yeah. Such a
legacy and such an impact.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:26.030
I love how you put those two together

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.211
when you're following your,

00:11:27.211 --> 00:11:28.970
putting your goals and
your passions together.

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:29.860
You know, I'm just thinking, you know,

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.040
in terms of some of our listeners

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:33.600
and just things I've heard from
people who might say, well,

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.190
I have these goals and these passions,

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:37.470
but I don't have any opportunities

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:40.180
to actually carry those things out

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.840
or are running into obstacles

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:44.230
even as you talked about trying
to have that discernment,

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:46.400
about knowing when to
push through something

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:48.660
if there's resistance, but
also knowing when to shift.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.810
So how have you done that in your life

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:51.960
when those obstacles have come up

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.920
and how would you give advice
to others who may be listening

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:56.500
who are saying, well, I have
these passions and goals,

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:59.350
but I just can't find a lane or an avenue

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.920
to really move that stuff forward.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.620
- Well, that's part of the deal, right?

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:04.680
That's part of the package.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.080
If you don't have a lane, if
you don't have an opportunity

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.860
after so many tries,
after so many attempts,

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:11.280
and there's no measure,

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.300
no metric that is scientifically rooted.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:17.440
You don't know if it's 10
years or 10 days, you know?

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.410
It's depending upon what your spirit is,

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:21.410
what your outlook is,
how much cash you got,

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:23.070
what you need to do in the meantime,

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.420
how many kids you got,
who you got to raise,

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:28.580
what parents, you need to
address their situation.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:33.580
So all of these existential
measures are indices,

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:39.280
are marks, are metrics that we deploy

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.600
to determine how much stick-to-it-iveness

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.863
we should display in a particular pursuit.

00:12:48.887 --> 00:12:49.830
You know, some people, you know,

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.700
Toni Morrison wasn't a professional writer

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.970
till she was what, 39 years old.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:56.320
Imagine had she given up,

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:00.490
we would have missed arguably
the greatest American writer

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.760
of her generation, the
greatest black writer ever.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:07.060
And we would have missed out on "Sula"

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:10.320
or we would've missed out on Milkman Dead.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:14.467
We would've missed out on, you know,

00:13:14.467 --> 00:13:18.130
"Beloved" and so on and
so forth and, you know,

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.400
we would have missed out on her genius.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.090
So thank God she was persistent,

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:23.500
but she had a job in the meantime.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.940
She was an editor at Random House.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:28.110
She was editing Angela Davis's work.

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.480
She was working with Toni Cade Bambara.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.950
So she was nurturing talent
as she was nurturing her own.

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.580
Her job was one thing,
her vocation another,

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.820
her vision yet another.

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:43.200
If you can find a space, as
I tell my sons and daughter,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.760
find something you would do
if nobody paid you a dime

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.300
and then figure out a way
to make money doing it

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.150
so you can support yourself,
so you can support your dream.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:55.520
So there's no exact
scientific calibration.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.210
There's no exact scientific
measure or metric

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:01.970
or meter or goal determiner.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:05.220
It's what you're made of and
it's what you're looking at

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.670
and it's how much you're
able to sustain yourself

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:12.471
in the midst of that and how
tenacious you are willing to be

00:14:12.471 --> 00:14:15.923
and persistent in the face of opposition,

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:18.580
in the face of opposition.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:22.530
So, you know, and in my case,

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:25.210
you know, I wanted to
write, I wanted to think,

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:28.380
I thought I was gonna be a minister,

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:32.040
I am a minister who was
a pastor, but I went,

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.300
I pastored three different churches,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:34.850
got kicked out of the third one

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.590
'cause I was trying to ordain three women

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:42.590
as deacons back in '83,
and that door closed on me.

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:47.520
Now I'm still committed to
gender justice in the church

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:51.230
but maybe the pastor, it
wasn't the path for me.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:54.260
And so when that door
closed rather precipitously,

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:57.840
you know, I began to focus
on becoming a scholar

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.360
and using my gifts that way

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:02.040
and maybe my ministry was in the classroom

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:06.150
and not in the pulpit alone or primarily.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.950
And so you get signals
and hints and indications,

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.900
again, how do you know
which one is for you or not?

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:16.650
It's an experimentation.
It's a trial and error.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.646
Sometimes trial and terror

00:15:18.646 --> 00:15:22.730
because the lack of
fulfillment can terrorize you,

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:27.090
can tantalize you, can traumatize you.

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:28.460
So you got to figure it out.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:29.780
You know, what are your resources?

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:32.290
What are your internal senses of things?

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:35.770
What are your intuitions
about what you're doing

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:37.920
and how much more you can take?

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.820
You know, we say God doesn't put anything

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:41.120
on you that you can't take,

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.470
any more on your plate
than you can consume

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:46.490
or any more weight on your
shoulders than you can bear.

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:49.030
Sometimes that's a tough, tough way to go.

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:52.460
But having said that, in my case,

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:55.460
testing, as the Bible
says, test the spirits,

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:58.300
testing out what my gifts and goals were

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:01.090
as I reshaped my goals
premised upon my gifts

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:04.750
or the particular moment
in which I was able

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:06.720
to realize those goals and ambitions,

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:09.860
'cause I had my goals and I had my gifts

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.190
but I didn't have, as you said,

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:12.970
the opportunity to realize them.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:16.070
What do I do in the meantime?
How do I realize it?

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:17.960
How do I recognize
there's more than one way

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:19.810
to get to a particular goal?

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:20.790
If you live in New York

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.100
and you're trying to get to
Broadway, there are many ways,

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.700
many more streets and avenues
to get there, to that street.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.870
So you've got to look at
alternative routes and the like.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:32.240
So when you put all that together,

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:35.900
it gives you a sense of how
much you're willing to invest,

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:37.540
how long you're willing to invest it,

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:40.310
how much you're capable
of sustaining yourself

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:43.470
in the midst of that, and
everything ain't for everybody.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:45.540
Every route is not for everybody else.

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:48.440
Your route is unique and
different than my route, so,

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:52.940
but if we have the same goal
of realizing our ambitions

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:54.790
and the willingness to stick to it,

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:58.460
sometimes that determines
even more than talent,

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:00.340
just the ability to sit
your butt in the seat

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:03.400
and keep writing, keep
thinking, keep talking,

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:06.930
keep doing what you do, just
outlasting everybody else

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.930
until your opportunity comes.

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:12.047
So you've got to be moved by the measure

00:17:12.047 --> 00:17:15.610
and the moment of the spirit within,

00:17:15.610 --> 00:17:19.290
the sense of within, the
intuition from within,

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.835
and the downright dogged determination

00:17:21.835 --> 00:17:25.150
to do what you gotta do until
you can reach your goals.

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.540
I mean, all of that is
a measure of what to do

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.940
and there's no scientific
way to tell anybody to do it.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:33.530
- Mm-hm, and I definitely appreciate that

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:35.185
even as a scientist, 'cause as you said,

00:17:35.185 --> 00:17:37.390
that's just not the way it flows,

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:40.650
and just that persistence in the passion

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:42.100
regardless of the doors closing

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:45.010
even as you were sharing
aspects of your story as well.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:46.110
But you touched a little bit

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:47.560
about the generational differences,

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.210
some of the positive
generational differences

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:51.900
in terms of really
incorporating that self-care

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:53.690
and having a balance in that way too.

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:56.030
From your lens and your perspective,

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.840
have you also seen any
generational differences

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:00.340
in terms of expectation?

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:01.173
'Cause you touched on that.

00:18:01.173 --> 00:18:03.870
You know, some of these things
didn't happen in a minute.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:07.870
They may have taken 10 months, 10 years.

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:09.970
And how do you keep that passion

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:11.257
while the prize isn't there?

00:18:11.257 --> 00:18:13.380
Not to break it down
too much generationally,

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.213
but I'm just curious from your, you know,

00:18:15.213 --> 00:18:16.930
from what you've seen over the years.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.790
- Yeah, no, I think there
are generational differences.

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:20.690
Some of us exaggerate them

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:24.340
because genre may be more
important than generation,

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:27.060
by which I mean if you were a
hustler a hundred years ago,

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:28.310
you might be a hustler today.

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.750
If you were tenacious a hundred years ago,

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.250
you might be tenacious today.

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.830
A certain kind of personality
that bucked the system,

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:36.170
you could have been Nat Turner then,

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:37.940
you could be Stacey Abrams now.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:42.800
So the genre, the measure,
the spirit, the approach,

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:47.593
the style of difference could
persist over space and time.

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:52.210
On the other hand, you could
be Frederick Douglas then,

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.833
you could be Malcolm X now, and so on.

00:18:56.810 --> 00:19:00.893
You could be Harriet Tubman then.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.070
You can be Sherrilyn Ifill now.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:05.610
Now, having said that,

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:08.580
I think there are
differences of generation

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.010
in terms of expectation,
'cause look at the load,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.830
the burden that's put on this generation.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:14.280
Can you imagine if we,

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:15.760
I mean, I didn't have Facebook coming up.

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:17.793
I had to face the book and read it.

00:19:17.793 --> 00:19:18.814
(both laughing)

00:19:18.814 --> 00:19:20.720
The only Facebook, face
that book, boy, and read it.

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.220
You know, I didn't have Twitter.

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:25.410
I was a-twitter. I was
excited about stuff.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:28.420
You know, swipe left,
swipe right. (laughs)

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:32.210
You know, what's that thing
they call when you do,

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:36.190
the dating service when you're
swiping and stuff, Tinder.

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:38.130
Yeah, we had to be tender in our emotions

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:41.040
toward the people we loved,
but we ain't had no Tinder

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.023
to figure out-
- You're more on top of this

00:19:42.023 --> 00:19:43.230
than I am, for God's sake. (laughs)

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:45.170
- Yeah, who to date.

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.150
I mean, social media is a game changer

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.363
and in many ways it's
been an extraordinary,

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:52.610
the internet has been extraordinary.

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:56.320
Stuff it took you, like, I
had to go to the lie-bury

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.000
to figure out where the lies were buried

00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:58.887
and dig them up.
- Oh, for goodness. (laughs)

00:19:58.887 --> 00:20:02.040
- Coming to books, you
had to go to the library,

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:05.010
the periodical readers, catalog, all that,

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:06.600
you have to go to the, man, you know,

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:09.280
to find out what article
was published in what year.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:12.360
Man, that was shoe
leather. That was pavement.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:16.350
That was physical geography and archeology

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:19.360
of knowledge in books,
which I love, I still do,

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.670
smelling them, going to bookstores

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.080
and libraries where you could just browse.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.090
I wasn't even looking for
that book and it popped up,

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:29.150
Professor Addy and what is going on

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:32.090
and I didn't even know about
that and that's amazing.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:35.650
Now you can browse even more reams

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.590
of gigs of data on the internet.

00:20:38.590 --> 00:20:41.020
Stuff that used to take
us, you know, weeks,

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:43.230
you can now have at your fingertips

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.220
and archives of knowledge
and digitized books

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:48.700
and libraries, it's phenomenal.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:53.030
On the other hand, man, there's,

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:55.200
the expectation is out of order.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:57.600
You know, you're gonna
be immediately successful

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.760
and if you're not, we're
going to note it on Twitter.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.760
You didn't do well and your
book didn't sell as much

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:07.610
and your work doesn't pay
off as quickly as others

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:09.530
'cause now you're measuring
not just the person

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:12.440
in your own neighborhood
or in your own classroom,

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:14.480
you're measuring yourself
against all of the people

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:16.620
on Twitter, all of the people on Facebook,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:18.837
all of the people on Instagram

00:21:18.837 --> 00:21:20.510
and you're looking at what they're doing

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:23.540
and you're figuring you ain't
got what they got and so on.

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:24.610
Now, it's not that keeping up

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:26.300
with the Joneses didn't prevail before,

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:28.300
but if you didn't have the Instagram

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:31.600
to see somebody posting
up, archiving their lives

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:34.380
in a way that ain't really the truth,

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:37.460
got filters on there, that ain't
really what they look like,

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:39.690
they got cellulite or cellu-heavy,

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:41.620
whatever they selling you anyway.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:46.620
So the point is all this stuff
now, filters and, you know,

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:49.980
stories and archiving your life

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:52.730
in a way that's not organic necessarily,

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:56.510
I'm not crapping on it,
it's another outlet,

00:21:56.510 --> 00:22:00.960
but dadgum, there are some
lethal deleterious consequences

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:02.200
to that as well.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.330
And also in terms of expectations,

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.050
people gonna be perfect and
live a certain kind of way.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:09.400
You sitting up on the
internet judging people,

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:12.870
you sitting up on Twitter
administering cancel culture,

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:14.930
I know it ain't popular to talk about it.

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:16.790
People say, oh, cancel
culture doesn't exist.

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:18.300
That's the right wing phenomenon.

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:19.690
They don't want to be held accountable.

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:22.933
That's true for many people
who are conservative,

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:25.990
or, not the same thing,

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:28.470
people who are racist
or sexist or transphobic

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.022
or homophobic and all that,

00:22:30.022 --> 00:22:31.230
they just don't want to be called out.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:32.600
So I get that.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:34.293
On the other hand,

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:38.860
there is something about
the self-righteousness

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:41.510
that you judge in other people
that you can't make a mistake

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.300
or if you make a mistake,
you can't overcome it

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:44.420
and acknowledge you did it.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:46.520
No, you're canceled. You're done.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:49.330
That's ludicrous. That's crazy.

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:50.500
- No room for growth.

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:51.740
- There is no room for growth

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:53.080
because you can't admit you messed up

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:55.790
and now nobody can admit
that they made an error

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:58.310
'cause if you admit you made an error,

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:02.120
then you can't say nothing
and you can't talk about it

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:03.930
and you can't open yourself up to it.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:08.930
There's no continuum. There's
no spectrum to be had here.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:10.710
There's no difference.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:13.390
You know, and some of
these feminists who are,

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:15.350
you know, talking about certain things,

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:17.040
be treating women worse than anybody else.

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:19.050
Some of these anti-racists
hate black people more

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:20.180
than anybody else.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.060
So I'm saying like, dogs, slow down

00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:23.190
with that kind of judgment

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:25.690
'cause I've seen you
from behind the scenes.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:28.290
I know the kind of
funky stuff you're doing

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:30.810
and, okay, you can grow, you can develop,

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:33.630
you can have an organic
relationship with truth

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:38.200
and, you know, evolution
of mind and so on.

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:40.070
And, you know, all of us need the space

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:43.000
to admit we messed up and
grow, but nobody can do it.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:44.580
Nobody can say they messed up now.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:48.760
Nobody can acknowledge that
they have made a mistake

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:50.550
because to acknowledge that mistake

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:54.040
is to immediately be judged
and to be done away with

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:58.930
and to have the swift, you
know, digital lynch mob descend

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:01.160
upon, you know, vulnerable people,

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:04.270
and we're all vulnerable
people, so we can never mess up.

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:06.220
We can never 'fess up and then dress up

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:08.300
and move on to the next situation.

00:24:08.300 --> 00:24:11.460
And I'm, you know, that
to me is destructive,

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:15.110
it's painful, and it's
problematic to the nth degree.

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:17.000
- Yeah, yeah. Totally agree with that.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:17.833
And I'm curious what you think

00:24:17.833 --> 00:24:19.990
on how we actually cultivate that

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:21.390
because that's such an important piece.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:22.810
I mean, I was saying
there's no room for growth.

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:24.158
We aren't perfect people.

00:24:24.158 --> 00:24:24.991
If we don't have room to grow,

00:24:24.991 --> 00:24:27.010
we're not gonna get through the challenges

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:28.040
and get to a place of healing,

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:29.540
and then even as I think about that

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.370
and tie that back into mental health,

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:33.660
not to say that mental
health challenges are an area

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:35.870
where people have messed up,
but it's an area of struggle.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:38.010
And so I think that same kind of feeling

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:40.150
comes into the mental health piece too

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:40.983
where people are struggling,

00:24:40.983 --> 00:24:42.630
there's no room to say I'm struggling

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:44.390
because that looks like imperfection.

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:45.760
So how do we actually cultivate that

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:48.720
and get to a healthier place
where we can all actually grow

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:52.220
and heal and move forward
and get off of this facade

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:53.710
as it were of having that-

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:55.130
- Yeah, no, it's a great point.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.550
I mean, stop lying to yourself

00:24:57.550 --> 00:25:00.370
and admit to yourself you
messed up, but you can't do it

00:25:00.370 --> 00:25:01.900
'cause if I admit to myself I messed up,

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.730
then I got imposter syndrome

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:05.510
and I don't think I belong there.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:07.190
And then mentally look
at what you're doing.

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:11.100
The mendacity, the lies,
the thick coat of lies.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:12.630
You're talking about self-help

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:15.240
or you're talking about,
you know, self-care,

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:16.550
how are you gonna care about yourself

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:17.900
if you can't tell yourself the truth?

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:20.260
But you can't tell yourself the truth

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:21.670
because if you tell yourself the truth

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:23.630
and you have to balance some
of the stuff that's going on,

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:25.740
then you'd have to tell
others that same truth

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:28.020
and you'd have to admit
that you ain't as perfect

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:29.670
as it appears that you are.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:33.820
And again, the social structure

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:36.100
then prevents individual consciousness,

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:40.110
the individuation that is a
measure of psychological health,

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:42.030
the ability to individuate,

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:45.540
to establish a basis of
individual ego-driven

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:48.340
in the best sense of that word recognition

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.270
and self-identification with those goals

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:52.340
and aspirations nurtured in you

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:54.890
that produce a healthy outcome,

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:57.710
a healthy ego, a healthy sense of self,

00:25:57.710 --> 00:25:59.780
whether you're dealing
with analytical psychology

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:01.810
of Karl Gustav Jung, or, you know,

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:03.880
the psychoanalytic framework of Freud

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:05.750
or the indirect approach of Rogers,

00:26:05.750 --> 00:26:09.300
or, you know, the best of
black psychological therapy

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:12.360
that joins a consciousness
of anti-blackness

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:15.610
to the kind of self-hatred
that might be uncritically

00:26:15.610 --> 00:26:18.410
or unconsciously internalized, whatever.

00:26:18.410 --> 00:26:20.260
At the end of the day, if
you can't acknowledge that,

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:22.670
if you can't fess up and
say dadgum it, I messed up,

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:25.830
or I'm not perfect and I need help,

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:27.250
it's a contradictory message.

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:29.060
On the one hand, self-care is about

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:30.500
I'm not well enough today.

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:32.970
I'm Naomi Osaka, I can't follow through,

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:37.278
I'm Simone Biles, I can't
follow through, and so on,

00:26:37.278 --> 00:26:39.930
and then if you admit
that, and then if you say,

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.140
but then, you know, part
of the blame could be

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:44.080
that I'm not ready, I didn't prepare,

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:47.000
I'm psychologically
not ready for the world

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:49.670
that is hurting me,
oh, what, what's wrong?

00:26:49.670 --> 00:26:53.630
So, you know, it's tough out
here to be able to do that.

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:56.970
So you've got to have
a world, create scenes,

00:26:56.970 --> 00:27:00.830
cosmoses, you know, worldviews,

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:04.810
intimate connections, communities,

00:27:04.810 --> 00:27:08.260
gatherings, organizations,

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:11.900
where you're able to share
that knowledge, be safe,

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:14.710
a safe place where you can tell the truth

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:17.140
and not be immediately judged.

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:18.760
And one person's safe space

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:21.130
is another person's vulnerability.

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:24.880
And even if people insist
on their having safe spaces,

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:29.880
they're developing those at
the expense of forcing others

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:32.800
to bow down before their narrow conception

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:36.320
of what is appropriate and
good and healthy and uplifting.

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:38.360
So we got to be honest about this stuff

00:27:38.360 --> 00:27:42.347
and stop holding each other
to unreasonable standards

00:27:42.347 --> 00:27:47.347
and unrighteous attempts to assault people

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:49.333
out of self-righteousness.

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:51.380
Now, yes, there's a way

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:52.840
in which you can hold
each other accountable

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:55.420
even as we recognize our faults and flaws.

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:57.110
it doesn't mean that
therefore anything goes

00:27:57.110 --> 00:27:59.193
and as long as I say I messed up

00:27:59.193 --> 00:28:00.960
that I don't have to be accountable.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:03.230
Yes, we all have to be held accountable.

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:04.430
There are things that I've done

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:08.340
of which I am enormously ashamed,
and I can say that openly.

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.410
But man, I can't really
get too specific about it

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:14.460
'cause if I get too specific,
then others, you know,

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:17.540
began to chime in and yap and hate on you

00:28:17.540 --> 00:28:20.260
while they're not admitting
the stuff that they did.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:22.150
You know, some sins are
more visible than others.

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:24.760
Some flaws are more evident than others.

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:28.980
Others are emotional sins or
internal psychological sins

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:31.900
that we commit against self or others.

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:36.350
Bad words, bad thoughts,
accompanying bad deeds.

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:39.150
So, you know, we got
to be extremely careful

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:43.280
and cautious about the
judgmentalism and the harshness

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:45.480
that goes in the name
of politically correct

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:49.670
or even woke ideology about
holding people to account.

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:52.630
If Martin Luther King Jr. could
forgive white supremacists

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:56.200
and racists and people who
were killing black people,

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:58.450
then we got to show some
more love to each other

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:00.430
than we showed to the white people

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.500
who tried to murder us back in the day

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:04.200
and some who are doing it today.

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:08.570
- Yeah, wow, there's a lot
there, so much that's important

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:10.700
and things that I think that
we can really pull apart.

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:12.180
One piece I wanted to
ask a little bit more

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:14.240
and kind of dig deep in is this idea

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:16.660
of individual versus community,

00:29:16.660 --> 00:29:18.040
'cause you talked about, you know,

00:29:18.040 --> 00:29:19.536
how we haven't paid attention to that

00:29:19.536 --> 00:29:21.280
in terms of our self-care, but I'm curious

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:25.060
just even in terms of the
historical context of that

00:29:25.060 --> 00:29:27.650
in this country and what
the, you know, such the,

00:29:27.650 --> 00:29:31.170
the emphasis on the community
suffering that was happening

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:33.600
and kind of the importance
of resilience is a word

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:35.060
that gets thrown around
for good or for bad,

00:29:35.060 --> 00:29:37.030
and, I mean, we could
dig into that as well,

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:39.190
but how do we balance those two?

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:42.032
Because in one sense, it
seems that the individual,

00:29:42.032 --> 00:29:44.760
individualism was squelched in order

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:46.770
so that we could thrive as a community.

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:48.110
At the same time, that could happen

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.160
at the expense of the individual.

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:52.970
So how do we balance that
in our current moment?

00:29:52.970 --> 00:29:54.670
Because there are even ways
that that happens today,

00:29:54.670 --> 00:29:57.740
even thinking about the
mental health aspect,

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:58.820
so thinking about some of the work

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:00.810
that David Williams has done at Harvard,

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:04.680
just showing the negative
mental health impacts

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.250
in black and brown communities in states

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:09.350
where individuals have been
killed at the hands of police,

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:11.456
unarmed individuals,
and the effect that has

00:30:11.456 --> 00:30:14.090
on the community and on the individual.

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:14.923
So I guess I'm trying to, you know,

00:30:14.923 --> 00:30:17.370
how do we tease those pieces apart?

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:20.070
Because they're both important
but there's consequences

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:23.960
of leaning too heavily
on one side or the other.

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:24.793
- Yeah, no doubt.

00:30:24.793 --> 00:30:27.472
I mean, David Williams,
Joy DeGruy, you know,

00:30:27.472 --> 00:30:30.543
when you think about
the post slave syndrome,

00:30:32.260 --> 00:30:34.920
post-slave traumatic syndrome,

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:39.220
where she speaks about the
trauma we endure collectively

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:40.923
as an enslaved people.

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:45.580
So, and brother Williams.

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:49.080
So yeah, the psychological consequence,

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:52.180
but they've also done
psychological studies of hope,

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:55.950
that people who hope
unrealistically suffer just as much.

00:30:55.950 --> 00:30:58.662
Like, ooh, so it's deep now.

00:30:58.662 --> 00:31:01.730
You get too much unrealistic
hope, you jacked up.

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:03.550
You're too pessimistic, you jacked up.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:05.152
You're too nihilistic, you jacked up.

00:31:05.152 --> 00:31:08.203
Dadgum, what's a brother to
do? What's a sister to do?

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:12.940
So the thing is, is that, yeah,
there are community traumas,

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:14.720
communities that are traumatized.

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:17.500
When we see black people who are subject

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:20.610
to the vicious, you know,

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:24.340
assault of a dominant
white supremacist society,

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.800
that traumatizes all of us

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.290
and our individuality is wiped away.

00:31:29.290 --> 00:31:32.580
The irony is the struggle
of the collective

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:35.200
is for the individual to be acknowledged.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:38.890
Don't treat us like that dadgum
parts of the same community

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:42.150
only in terms of your stereotype.

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:44.070
All black people are lazy.

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:46.660
All black people, you know,

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:50.270
are unfaithful to the task at hand, right?

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:52.450
All black people are,
and fill in the blanks.

00:31:52.450 --> 00:31:53.710
Everything you say about black people

00:31:53.710 --> 00:31:55.640
is true about everybody, every group,

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:57.430
but you begin to stereotype

00:31:57.430 --> 00:32:00.560
and rigorously, or, you
know, don't apply rigor

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:04.260
to your deconstruction of those practices,

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:08.330
dispositions, habits, and inclinations

00:32:08.330 --> 00:32:12.253
of black people to
behave in a certain way.

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:17.520
And that they're not just black,
everybody does that, right?

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:20.380
Every community that is poor will engage

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:21.970
in criminal activity,

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:24.730
every community whose back
is against the wall, right?

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:27.700
Poles, Jews, Italians,
you know, in this country

00:32:27.700 --> 00:32:29.460
who were in the shadow and the ghetto

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:31.753
before African people were in America.

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:36.740
At the same time, the unique
condition of being enslaved

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:40.670
in this country and then being, you know,

00:32:40.670 --> 00:32:42.500
then having the force and power

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:46.020
of the state turned against
us and denying our humanity,

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:47.107
that has to be acknowledged too,

00:32:47.107 --> 00:32:49.760
and the psychological
consequences of that.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:52.250
So yes, there's a
psychological consequence

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:53.960
to being treated as a group,

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:55.740
all black people, and fill in the blanks,

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:57.300
all black people, fill in the blanks,

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.460
all black people, fill in
the blanks, the stereotypes,

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:02.400
the narrow conceptions, and a stereotype

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:04.210
is a lazy person's way of
dealing with the other.

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:06.770
Some of it, some small
part of it could be true,

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:10.850
but the vast majority of the
data rests upon a refusal

00:33:10.850 --> 00:33:13.140
to acknowledge the person before me

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:14.290
and to put the group before,

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:17.743
all Jews, all Poles, all
Italians, all Lithuanians,

00:33:19.747 --> 00:33:22.140
and all Polish and blah, blah, blah,

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:24.260
you know, do X, Y, and Z,

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:27.310
as opposed to dealing with
the individual person,

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:30.650
Jewish, Italian, Polish, you know,

00:33:30.650 --> 00:33:34.910
Korean, before me, while at the same time,

00:33:34.910 --> 00:33:36.660
acknowledging that there are group traits

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:38.850
that are developed as a result

00:33:38.850 --> 00:33:42.760
of having imposed upon those
groups certain restrictions

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:44.770
so that a group
consciousness is developed.

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:46.060
But you mad at us,

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:48.447
why all you black people got
to come together as a group?

00:33:48.447 --> 00:33:50.260
Why can't you be treated as an individual?

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:51.140
That's when they want.

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:53.610
They don't want to deal
with the racism question,

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:55.870
the structural characteristics of racism.

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:57.260
Well, be treated as an individual.

00:33:57.260 --> 00:33:58.870
But you don't treat us as individuals.

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:02.060
You continually treat us as
members of a particular group

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:04.930
that you stereotype or
you're prejudiced against.

00:34:04.930 --> 00:34:06.830
So you go up to a black
person you don't even know

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:08.740
and hate them because they are black.

00:34:08.740 --> 00:34:11.080
That ain't got nothing to
do with the individual data,

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.810
experience, engagement with that person.

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:16.190
You're treating us as a collective.

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:18.310
So it's collective when it's convenient.

00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:23.310
It's individual when it's
to the delight or benefit

00:34:24.330 --> 00:34:29.120
of that particular person
or community assessing us.

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:32.000
So yeah, you got the individual
stuff on the one hand

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:34.720
where we're treated as individuals,

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:37.640
exceptions from the
group, that's problematic.

00:34:37.640 --> 00:34:39.960
You're better than,
Dr. Addy, you're better

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:43.780
than most of the other people
that I've met of your color

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:46.840
and you got a better
character and you're more,

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:49.760
you're hard-working and go and praise you

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:52.070
at the expense of your group

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:56.390
through the means of a stereotype
that dishonors the group

00:34:56.390 --> 00:35:00.830
and therefore exaggerates
or distorts the individual.

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:04.330
So yeah, that stuff is tough, no doubt,

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:06.560
and there are issues on both sides

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:09.380
as a collective, as an individual.

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:10.760
And we've got to confront those.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:14.140
We've got to acknowledge
our group solidarity

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:15.640
and we've got to acknowledge our quest

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.040
for individual expression

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:19.800
so that we don't impose those
restraints on each other.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:21.810
You know, you ain't really
black 'cause you scuba dive.

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:23.610
You know, you ain't really
black 'cause you're trans.

00:35:23.610 --> 00:35:25.470
No, you ain't really
black 'cause you're queer.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:27.960
No, you ain't really black
'cause you're Afrofuturist.

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:30.380
You ain't really black because
you like country music.

00:35:30.380 --> 00:35:33.310
You ain't really black because
you only listen to Beethoven

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:36.730
and not Busta Rhymes and on and on and on.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:40.330
So we impose these constraints,
impose these narrow limits

00:35:40.330 --> 00:35:41.450
on ourselves as well.

00:35:41.450 --> 00:35:44.440
Now, that's not to say
that those who operate

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:46.440
within our communities who are hurting

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:49.320
and harming black people, who self-hate,

00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:54.200
and as a result of that,
other-hate, that's real too.

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:56.640
But everybody who's not part
of the group in the main

00:35:56.640 --> 00:36:00.260
is not a self-hater and
doesn't want to undermine

00:36:00.260 --> 00:36:03.450
the capacity of black people
to move forward as a community.

00:36:03.450 --> 00:36:05.820
They want to argue for some oxygen,

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:09.520
to breathe the air that they
feel most comfortable inhaling.

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:11.100
So it's a constant battle.

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:13.450
It's a constant pivoting between,

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:17.240
toggling between the
collective and the individual,

00:36:17.240 --> 00:36:21.800
but the corrosive consequences of both,

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:23.567
of seeing our black people traumatized

00:36:23.567 --> 00:36:26.630
and that regardless of our, you know,

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:30.710
our economic status or our racial beliefs

00:36:30.710 --> 00:36:34.870
or our religious orientation
or our sexual orientation,

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:37.810
on the one hand, then there's
the stuff we do to each other

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:41.450
to impose narrow constraints
and judgments on each other

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:44.830
and it's what we have to
put up with in a society

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:46.490
that wants to touch our hair,

00:36:46.490 --> 00:36:51.000
that wants to believe that
microaggressions are harmless,

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:54.300
the Karens of the world,
to dictate where we can eat

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:57.980
and not eat, sell water
and not sell water,

00:36:57.980 --> 00:37:00.703
lemonade, not lemonade,
barbecue, not barbecue,

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:05.500
go into, you know, a coffee shop

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:08.770
and be treated as a thug and the like,

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:12.150
or at a university and
be seen as out of place.

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.200
So all of that stuff
we're constantly trying

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:18.780
to negotiate between, and
it's even more havoc wreaked

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:22.500
when we impose those
narrow vicious beliefs

00:37:22.500 --> 00:37:23.693
on each other as well.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:26.401
- Yeah, yeah, that's so well said

00:37:26.401 --> 00:37:28.030
because of all the
tensions between the two,

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:30.610
as you mentioned, you know, I'm curious

00:37:30.610 --> 00:37:32.800
from your perspective as a sociologist

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:34.750
and just all the other
hats that you wear too,

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:37.360
'cause you talked about,
you know, all the things,

00:37:37.360 --> 00:37:39.320
all the risks of being a
black person in this country,

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:40.153
as you've just gone through,

00:37:40.153 --> 00:37:42.430
and I know you mentioned that
in your convocation address

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:44.610
at Meharry Medical College as well.

00:37:44.610 --> 00:37:49.610
How do you see that impacting
our mental health in general

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:51.110
and just our outlook in the world

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:53.290
and how do we actually
try and move through that,

00:37:53.290 --> 00:37:55.450
you know, not necessarily
maybe from a mental health lens

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:57.680
or psychiatric or psychological lens,

00:37:57.680 --> 00:37:59.330
but from your perspective
as a sociologist, like,

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.210
what are healthy ways
that we can actually,

00:38:01.210 --> 00:38:03.390
one, how does it impact us
and what are healthy ways

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:05.560
we can move through that to still thrive

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.290
and not have this false sense
of hope that you talked about?

00:38:08.290 --> 00:38:12.920
- Yeah, well, you know, you
want to have as clear a concept

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:15.090
of your capacities as possible

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:17.310
so that you can have
a realistic assessment

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:19.480
of your likely trajectory of success

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:20.950
and achievement in the world.

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:24.360
You got to figure out ways to
have a realistic assessment

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:27.010
of self in regard to obstacles

00:38:27.010 --> 00:38:29.873
that might prevent the
flourishing of that self

00:38:29.873 --> 00:38:32.400
and therefore negotiate a world

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:34.840
that is constantly throwing up obstacles

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:37.920
and you're constantly, through resilience,

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:40.540
trying to overcome them,
trying to engage them,

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:44.910
but also use them as grist
for your meal as a story

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:48.550
in your larger narrative
of success or overcoming.

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:51.760
So you gotta be as cunning and as clever

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:54.887
and as crafty as our ancestors, you know,

00:38:54.887 --> 00:38:57.587
faking like they liked stuff
when they didn't like it,

00:38:58.452 --> 00:39:01.010
you know, yes sir, master,
when they were like, mm-hm,

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:03.070
giving them the bird behind the scenes,

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:07.130
or putting, shall we say,
urination into their water

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:08.880
to make it taste better,

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:11.110
finding everyday forms of resistance,

00:39:11.110 --> 00:39:13.970
aborting babies sometimes
from enslavement,

00:39:13.970 --> 00:39:15.810
breaking tools and implements,

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:18.020
hey, it don't work,
what you want me to do?

00:39:18.020 --> 00:39:22.300
Breaking the legs of animals
so that they couldn't,

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:27.010
you know, be used and
deployed and enslaved to work.

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:29.740
So they were all kind of crafty, cunning,

00:39:29.740 --> 00:39:34.650
creative, clever ways
we found to negotiate.

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:36.567
Now, I don't want any
animals to be hurting.

00:39:36.567 --> 00:39:40.210
I don't want any tools to
be broken unnecessarily,

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:42.220
but I'm talking about
an approach, a mindset,

00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:46.330
a mental health strategy
that says that at all costs,

00:39:46.330 --> 00:39:49.220
I must maintain my belief
that God has blessed me,

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:51.720
the universe has blessed me,
if you don't believe in God,

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:55.510
that I am who I am as a
result of the talent I have,

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:58.380
I should be able to recognize that talent

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:03.380
in a goal-oriented society
that throws up impediments

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:07.483
and, you know, things that
prevent my flourishing.

00:40:08.900 --> 00:40:10.570
I've got to constantly fight against that.

00:40:10.570 --> 00:40:12.110
I got to fight against the self-doubt

00:40:12.110 --> 00:40:13.400
that has been implanted in me

00:40:13.400 --> 00:40:18.020
not because of an inherent
sense of inferiority,

00:40:18.020 --> 00:40:22.000
but by an imposed one
that has been inherited

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:24.850
from a dominant white supremacist logic

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:27.990
that continues to constrain who I am

00:40:27.990 --> 00:40:31.090
or a sense of self in the midst of that.

00:40:31.090 --> 00:40:34.950
So mental health, you got
to, you know, maintain,

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:36.950
you know, like, look, I'm worthy.

00:40:36.950 --> 00:40:38.110
I'm good.

00:40:38.110 --> 00:40:40.610
It's all right to acknowledge
my vulnerabilities

00:40:40.610 --> 00:40:42.040
and my faults and my flaws,

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:46.240
but also to stand up and to
celebrate my achievements,

00:40:46.240 --> 00:40:48.540
the vast region of possibility

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:51.036
that I have barely begun to tap,

00:40:51.036 --> 00:40:54.280
that is open to me and I must insist

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:57.373
on realizing that ambition.

00:40:58.330 --> 00:41:00.400
That's a mentally healthy thing to do.

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:02.710
You know, the new movie
"King Richard" is coming out

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:06.530
where Richard Williams, the
father of Venus and Serena,

00:41:06.530 --> 00:41:08.700
had to fight a bunch of stereotypes.

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:11.350
He's crazy. He's got two daughters.

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:12.310
What does he know?

00:41:12.310 --> 00:41:14.430
He has no experience with tennis

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:17.270
and he's in Compton and
the concrete jungle.

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:18.220
What the hell does he know?

00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:21.160
Well, enough to produce two of
the greatest tennis players,

00:41:21.160 --> 00:41:24.970
one, arguably the greatest
tennis player ever bar gender,

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:28.030
another one, one of the
greatest of all time in Venus.

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:32.410
So yeah, he was crazy like a fox mentally,

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:35.640
spiritually, emotionally,
against the grain,

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:38.640
looking unorthodox, sometimes
we have to be unorthodox.

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:40.680
Sometimes we have to be on the margins.

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:44.720
We can't be in the main, the main stream.

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:46.950
You have to flow in
the way and the cadence

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:48.980
that has been given to you,

00:41:48.980 --> 00:41:52.380
the moral ambitions that
have been nurtured in you,

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:56.480
the styles, the measures and means

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:59.660
that are most akin to your flourishing,

00:41:59.660 --> 00:42:02.023
that don't hurt or harm others.

00:42:02.023 --> 00:42:05.180
So in terms of mental health, yeah.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:08.180
You know, you got to figure
out ways to affirm self,

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:11.980
to love self, to embrace
self as other selves

00:42:11.980 --> 00:42:14.840
creatively engage in the same process,

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:17.841
fighting for opportunities, opening doors,

00:42:17.841 --> 00:42:22.270
not feeling that your success is harmed

00:42:22.270 --> 00:42:25.960
if another person's success is present,

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:30.920
a lot of things we can do to
reinforce our sense of self.

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:33.490
Go to church, go to
temple, go to synagogue,

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:36.820
read a religious book,
have a set of affirmations,

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:41.320
have goals that are articulated
on refrigerator doors

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:42.670
and on and on and on.

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:45.220
Read, you know, the Bhagavad Gita,

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:47.250
the Holy Quran, the Tao Te Ching,

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:49.400
whatever it is that floats your boat,

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:52.970
Superman, Batman, comic books, you know,

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:56.640
rap lyrics, Lil Wayne, Dr. Dre,

00:42:56.640 --> 00:43:00.010
Jay-Z, Lauryn Hill, you know,

00:43:00.010 --> 00:43:02.780
whatever it takes to sustain you.

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:05.840
Listen to Anita Baker or Luther Vandross

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:08.210
or whatever it is that uplifts you,

00:43:08.210 --> 00:43:10.830
that gives you the confidence and courage

00:43:10.830 --> 00:43:13.840
and conviction to move forward

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:18.270
and have a strong self-concept,

00:43:18.270 --> 00:43:22.120
a mentally healthy
outlook that keeps at bay

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:24.480
the demons of depression and distress

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:27.990
and anxiety that eat away at the fabric

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:31.400
of your self-confidence in this world.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:33.450
- Mm, so well said, and
I love all the pieces

00:43:33.450 --> 00:43:35.113
that you pulled in 'cause
there's so much of that

00:43:35.113 --> 00:43:37.960
that ties into the brain
science and the neuroscience

00:43:37.960 --> 00:43:40.530
of what we need moving forward
to even, as you talked about,

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:42.950
the expectation that we have for ourselves

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:46.350
or the importance of community
or the importance of music

00:43:46.350 --> 00:43:48.380
and how that really engages, you know,

00:43:48.380 --> 00:43:50.250
reward pathways in our
brains that are important

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:51.870
for helping us kind of move through things

00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:52.840
on a day-to-day basis.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:55.000
So it was great hearing that, you know,

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:57.340
that perspective from you as a minister,

00:43:57.340 --> 00:43:58.680
a sociologist and professor,

00:43:58.680 --> 00:44:00.870
and how that ties in so
strongly to neuroscience

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:03.860
about what it really means for our brains

00:44:03.860 --> 00:44:05.670
and helps us to move through
some of these challenges

00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:07.420
in a lot of ways as well.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.810
And again, it loops back to
what you mentioned early on,

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:14.150
I think too, about that
balance between the self-care

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:16.280
and then also kind of
moving through things

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:18.850
but not trying to sway one
way or the other, but, I mean,

00:44:18.850 --> 00:44:20.280
the way that you kind
of painted that picture

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:21.900
for really having a holistic balance

00:44:21.900 --> 00:44:24.550
and really having those pieces
baked in, as you mentioned,

00:44:24.550 --> 00:44:26.350
I think, is so powerful.

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:28.063
And I imagine that, you know,

00:44:28.063 --> 00:44:30.310
even if it hasn't been
something that you've stated

00:44:30.310 --> 00:44:32.710
throughout your long career,

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:34.410
it seems like you have
those pieces in place

00:44:34.410 --> 00:44:37.790
to really kind of keep those
pieces in check in a sense

00:44:37.790 --> 00:44:39.410
which I think is really-
- Yes sir, no, no, no,

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:41.750
well-stated, eloquent,
more eloquently than I did,

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:44.990
but yes, sir, and those
neuropathways that are developed,

00:44:44.990 --> 00:44:47.290
those endorphins that are released,

00:44:47.290 --> 00:44:51.900
the cycle chemical, cycle
neurochemical possibilities

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:52.733
that are unleashed.

00:44:52.733 --> 00:44:56.610
If it's good for your soul,
it's good for your endorphins.

00:44:56.610 --> 00:44:58.140
It's good for the biology.

00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:00.450
It's good for the neuro-transmitters

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:04.970
that carry messages of hope
and aspiration and possibility

00:45:04.970 --> 00:45:09.970
without being, you know,
narrowly retrofitted as positive

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:12.850
as opposed to deeply rooted in hope,

00:45:12.850 --> 00:45:17.610
'cause hope confronts the
negative, confronts the horrible,

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:21.280
confronts the terrorizing,
the traumatizing,

00:45:21.280 --> 00:45:25.886
and then gives answer to those pathways

00:45:25.886 --> 00:45:29.511
that portend nuclear
explosion in your brain

00:45:29.511 --> 00:45:33.160
and redirect them towards
something more powerful

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:37.658
and uplifting and beautiful
and edifying at the same time.

00:45:37.658 --> 00:45:40.530
- Oh, totally agreed. Full
circle in a lot of ways.

00:45:40.530 --> 00:45:41.740
I know we're running short on time

00:45:41.740 --> 00:45:43.060
but I did want to give you a chance

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:45.470
just to talk about your
upcoming book as well

00:45:45.470 --> 00:45:47.140
'cause I know that's gonna
be released very soon,

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:52.140
and what should readers expect
from this latest gifting?

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:54.057
- Well, thank you, sir.

00:45:54.057 --> 00:45:56.610
"Entertaining Race: Performing
Blackness in America"

00:45:56.610 --> 00:45:58.330
is a kind of collection
of stuff I've been doing

00:45:58.330 --> 00:46:00.450
for nearly the last 30 years.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:05.013
Sermons, essays, reviews, film,

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:10.773
you know, interviews with me and others,

00:46:11.660 --> 00:46:15.290
commencement speeches, protest speeches,

00:46:15.290 --> 00:46:17.350
op-eds, every genre.

00:46:17.350 --> 00:46:20.360
I've got a restless
fascination with literacy

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:24.740
and expression and how to articulate ideas

00:46:24.740 --> 00:46:29.023
and aspirations that are
not limited to one genre.

00:46:30.630 --> 00:46:34.300
And so people will get a chance
to peek into my own spirit,

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:38.690
my mind, my soul, my
desires, my ambitions,

00:46:38.690 --> 00:46:41.970
my thoughts, and see me reckoning

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:45.880
and wrestling with them
out loud in public spaces

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:49.603
that allow others to be
challenged, to be championed,

00:46:51.540 --> 00:46:53.230
and hopefully to be uplifted

00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:58.280
at the same time to do their own thing,

00:46:58.280 --> 00:47:01.230
to express themselves in
ways that are appropriate

00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:03.000
and uplifting not only to themselves,

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:05.610
but to the communities around them.

00:47:05.610 --> 00:47:06.500
- Well, I look forward to reading.

00:47:06.500 --> 00:47:08.190
I mean, even the way you
just characterized that,

00:47:08.190 --> 00:47:10.380
I love the fact that
it has that wrestling,

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:11.690
the wrestling and the hope together

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:13.080
'cause as we were talking,
that seems, I mean,

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:16.080
that's the key piece so that
we don't get in this facade

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:17.820
of not having to work through things

00:47:17.820 --> 00:47:20.170
to get to a place of hope and healing, so.

00:47:20.170 --> 00:47:22.210
- Yes, sir. Thank you, my brother.

00:47:22.210 --> 00:47:24.040
- Well, I appreciate you
taking the time again

00:47:24.040 --> 00:47:26.610
to be on the "Addie Hour"
and this is a conversation

00:47:26.610 --> 00:47:28.150
I think a lot of people
are gonna go back to

00:47:28.150 --> 00:47:30.970
and probably listen to a few
times to catch all the nuances

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:33.220
and the nuggets that
you dropped throughout.

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:35.520
So really appreciate your time with us

00:47:35.520 --> 00:47:37.810
and just for joining this conversation.

00:47:37.810 --> 00:47:38.643
- Thanks for having me.

00:47:38.643 --> 00:47:40.810
Keep up that extremely important work

00:47:40.810 --> 00:47:44.710
of keeping us on the right
path of mental health,

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:47.900
mental wellbeing, emotional security

00:47:47.900 --> 00:47:49.260
in the midst of vulnerability.

00:47:49.260 --> 00:47:50.960
Thank you so much for the
work you do, my friend.

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:52.410
- Of course. Oh, my pleasure.

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:54.570
Glad to contribute in a meaningful way.

00:47:54.570 --> 00:47:55.710
- Thank you, sir.

